WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Welcome to Grade 4 English, Unit 3: Food and Health.

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.960
Food is one of Allah's great blessings.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.080
In this unit you will learn how to
talk about food and health in English.

4
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.740
You will read about where food comes
from, how to keep it clean and safe,

5
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.780
and why fresh food helps your body and your brain.

6
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:26.380
You will learn to give clear instructions, ask good
questions, and compare healthy and unhealthy choices.

7
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:32.400
Along the way you will meet many new words —
from sandwich and rice to pesticide and famine —

8
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.780
and you will use grammar that helps you
explain rules, reasons, and comparisons.

9
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.520
By the end, you will be ready to read
this unit's story, “The Bitter Lunch”,

10
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.940
about a boy named Musa who learns to check his food with care.

11
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:53.240
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

12
00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.920
By the end of this unit, you will be able to name and
describe many foods, and talk about where food comes from.

13
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.740
you will also explain why fresh, clean
food is good for your body and your brain.

14
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.820
you will also give clear instructions
using imperatives (“wash the fruit.”).

15
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.600
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

16
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.360
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
sandwich, lamb, rice, spice, bakery, fresh.

17
00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:27.360
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

18
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.320
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

19
00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:36.260
The reading sequence includes From
Farm to Plate and A Poster on the Wall.

20
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:37.740
Read actively.

21
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.740
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

22
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.040
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

23
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.680
The first language focus is Modal Verbs — must and might.

24
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.380
Modal verbs are special helping verbs.

25
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.240
In this unit we use two of them: must and might.

26
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.100
Must shows that something is
necessary or very important — a rule.

27
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.500
Might shows that something is possible, but not certain.

28
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.400
Knowing both helps you explain
rules and possibilities clearly.

29
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:17.140
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

30
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.860
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: My Favourite Meal.

31
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:27.020
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

32
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.080
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

33
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.600
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

34
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.420
You are ready to begin.

35
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.620
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

36
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.380
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

37
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.200
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
